1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,409
Welcome to agentic dbt development.

2
00:00:03,689 --> 00:00:13,460
In this crash course we build one real dbt project with an AI agent, from an empty folder to production patterns, using Rosetta dbt Studio.

3
00:00:13,739 --> 00:00:15,702
All you need is basic SQL.

4
00:00:16,802 --> 00:00:19,234
Agentic means the AI does more than chat.

5
00:00:19,514 --> 00:00:23,525
It reads your project, writes files, and runs dbt commands.

6
00:00:23,805 --> 00:00:27,239
That is powerful, which is exactly why you stay the engineer.

7
00:00:27,519 --> 00:00:28,863
The agent proposes.

8
00:00:29,144 --> 00:00:32,642
You review, you decide, and dbt tells you if it is right.

9
00:00:33,742 --> 00:00:36,601
Every time we use the agent, we follow one loop.

10
00:00:36,881 --> 00:00:38,353
Prompt with good context.

11
00:00:38,633 --> 00:00:39,955
Let the agent plan.

12
00:00:40,236 --> 00:00:41,153
Let it act.

13
00:00:41,433 --> 00:00:42,948
Then review every change.

14
00:00:43,227 --> 00:00:46,428
Then run dbt, and verify the actual rows.

15
00:00:46,708 --> 00:00:50,121
Review is the step people skip, and the one that matters most.

16
00:00:51,221 --> 00:00:53,333
Six habits, used in every section.

17
00:00:53,613 --> 00:00:55,682
Give the agent files, not memory.

18
00:00:55,962 --> 00:00:57,968
Plan before acting on big tasks.

19
00:00:58,248 --> 00:00:59,570
One task per chat.

20
00:00:59,850 --> 00:01:01,813
Read every diff before you keep it.

21
00:01:02,093 --> 00:01:03,778
Let dbt be the judge.

22
00:01:04,058 --> 00:01:07,514
And if you correct the agent twice, write the rule into a skill.

23
00:01:08,614 --> 00:01:10,748
Each section follows the same rhythm.

24
00:01:11,027 --> 00:01:12,692
A few slides with the idea.

25
00:01:12,972 --> 00:01:16,086
The official dbt documentation, version one.

26
00:01:16,366 --> 00:01:22,873
Then a recorded demo in Rosetta dbt Studio, where we build it, first by hand and then with the agent.

27
00:01:23,153 --> 00:01:24,838
We finish with a git checkpoint.

28
00:01:25,938 --> 00:01:27,794
This is the project you will build.

29
00:01:28,074 --> 00:01:29,866
Sources and seeds on the left.

30
00:01:30,146 --> 00:01:35,181
Staging models, one intermediate model with our business rules, and marts on the right.

31
00:01:35,461 --> 00:01:41,477
A snapshot keeps history at the bottom, and an exposure marks the real analysis we answer at the end.

32
00:01:42,577 --> 00:01:49,404
We use real data: New York City yellow taxi trips, published every month by the Taxi and Limousine Commission.

33
00:01:49,684 --> 00:01:55,550
Millions of rows per month, small enough for a laptop, and messy enough that our tests will really fail.

34
00:01:55,830 --> 00:01:56,897
That is the point.

35
00:01:57,997 --> 00:02:00,408
The screen in Rosetta dbt Studio.

36
00:02:00,688 --> 00:02:02,416
The project files on the left.

37
00:02:02,696 --> 00:02:06,237
The editor in the middle, with run, preview and compiled SQL.

38
00:02:06,517 --> 00:02:07,989
The AI chat on the right.

39
00:02:08,269 --> 00:02:12,472
Every file the agent writes opens for review, with Keep or Discard.

40
00:02:12,752 --> 00:02:15,738
Every command it wants to run waits for Allow or Deny.

41
00:02:16,838 --> 00:02:18,182
The chat has two modes.

42
00:02:18,462 --> 00:02:19,913
Ask mode is read-only.

43
00:02:20,193 --> 00:02:23,500
We use it to explain, profile data, and trace lineage.

44
00:02:23,780 --> 00:02:27,321
Code mode is where the agent writes files and runs commands.

45
00:02:27,601 --> 00:02:29,201
Ask when you want to understand.

46
00:02:29,481 --> 00:02:31,188
Code when you want something built.

47
00:02:32,287 --> 00:02:35,573
The dbt documentation now has two versions.

48
00:02:35,853 --> 00:02:40,546
This course follows version one, the version Rosetta dbt Studio runs.

49
00:02:40,826 --> 00:02:45,605
The core idea: dbt transforms data that is already in your database.

50
00:02:45,885 --> 00:02:51,303
You write select statements, and dbt handles the order, the tests and the documentation.

51
00:02:52,403 --> 00:02:53,427
Here is the map.

52
00:02:53,707 --> 00:03:01,025
Part one covers loading data, project structure, sources, seeds, staging, tests, macros, and the final marts.

53
00:03:01,305 --> 00:03:08,515
Part two covers unit tests, incremental models, snapshots, and documentation, ending with a real analysis.

54
00:03:09,615 --> 00:03:24,271
In the demo for this section, we create a new project called N Y C taxi, connect it to a new DuckDB database file, look at the AI, skills and M C P settings, and run dbt debug to check that everything works.
